# Line endings.
#
# ★ WHY THIS EXISTS, AND WHY IT IS THE SAME FIX AS IN Bannerlord-Helper.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` makes git store LF in the repository but write CRLF into the working
# tree. The rewrite wrote 13 source files with CRLF directly, so those files differed from
# the other 79 in a way that has nothing to do with their content.
#
# Unlike Helper - where a linter reads the working tree and turned this into ~1120 reported
# errors - this repository has no linter configured, so nothing failed. That is the worse
# case, not the better one: an inconsistency that no check reports is one that grows. The
# next person to touch one of these files produces a diff full of line-ending noise, and
# concludes the file was largely rewritten when it was not.
#
# Pinning the endings makes the working tree and the repository agree, so a diff shows what
# actually changed. Applied per file kind rather than as a blanket `*`, so binary assets
# (png, zip, dll) are left untouched - a blanket rule would corrupt them.

*.sh   text eol=lf
*.ts   text eol=lf
*.mjs  text eol=lf
*.js   text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.xml  text eol=lf
*.cs   text eol=lf
*.go   text eol=lf
